WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
Kubernetes basics.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:06.250
Pods, deployments and services all rest on one big idea.

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.467
Let's start there.

4
00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:12.000
Kubernetes is built on declarative desired state.

5
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.345
You do not say start a container.

6
00:00:14.345 --> 00:00:18.000
You say: three replicas of this image, behind this address.

7
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.900
Then controllers work to close any gap between that and reality.

8
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.033
Controllers run one loop, forever.

9
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:26.967
First, observe reality.

10
00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:29.322
Second, diff it against your spec.

11
00:00:29.322 --> 00:00:33.433
Say a node died and took a pod with it, so two does not equal three.

12
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.600
Third, act to close the gap.

13
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.600
Self-healing is not a feature.

14
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.867
It is the loop's normal operation.

15
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.200
Now the three objects you will use daily.

16
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.394
A pod is the smallest unit, one or more containers sharing network and storage.

17
00:00:48.394 --> 00:00:50.933
It is ephemeral, so never point at a pod.

18
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:57.200
A deployment manages a set of identical pods, handling scaling, rolling updates and healing.

19
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.467
A service gives a stable name that load-balances across whichever pods currently match its labels.

20
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:07.433
Here is a deployment, trimmed to the important lines.

21
00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:10.133
Replicas three is the desired state.

22
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:15.067
The labels connect the pods to this deployment, and to any service that selects them.

23
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:18.268
And this is the container image each pod runs.

24
00:01:18.268 --> 00:01:21.800
Delete a pod on purpose, and watch a replacement appear.

25
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.167
Here is the payoff.

26
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:29.867
A memory leak gets one pod's container OOM-killed most nights around two in the morning.

27
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:32.752
Before Kubernetes, that was a pager alert.

28
00:01:32.752 --> 00:01:38.600
Now the kubelet restarts the container in place within seconds, and its restart count ticks up.

29
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.537
The service stops sending traffic to the pod while it restarts.

30
00:01:42.537 --> 00:01:46.867
You find out from the restart counter, and fix the leak calmly over coffee.

31
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:48.842
Let's recap.

32
00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:52.267
You declare desired state, and controllers reconcile.

33
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:53.833
A pod is ephemeral.

34
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:57.067
A deployment keeps the right number of replicas healthy.

35
00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:00.778
And a service is a stable name over those pods.

36
00:02:00.778 --> 00:02:01.867
Next up: Nginx.
